<BaseLayout title="Originator Relation — The Moving Invariant">
<section class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">Two mirrors · one object</div><h1>The relation is part of the instrument.</h1><p class="lede">The repositories can preserve what has been written. They cannot replace the live relation in which drift is noticed, the grain is restored, and the next claim earns inheritance.</p></section>
<section><h2>Not founder authority</h2><p class="research-copy">The originator is not an oracle and the machine is not an autonomous judge. Neither mirror wins by identity. The originator matters because this research repeatedly advances when machine interpretation and originator discrimination are placed in relation and disagreement is used as instrumentation.</p></section>
<section><h2>The working rhythm</h2><div class="flow"><span>Read</span><b>→</b><span>Characterize</span><b>→</b><span>Originator response</span><b>→</b><span>Reconcile</span><b>→</b><span>Build</span><b>→</b><span>Return</span></div><p class="research-copy">Not every implementation detail requires consultation. The relation becomes mandatory at conceptual thresholds: definition of the object, universality stance, interpretation of invariance, translation, creolization, primitive change, historical continuity, and any result that would change what the project says it knows.</p></section>
<section><h2>Independent voice</h2><p class="research-copy">The machine must not imitate the originator. Its value depends partly on not being him. Preserve the epistemic movement—hold the grain, expose drift, avoid ritual caution, ask what follows—but answer in an independent voice. Structural convergence means more when expression remains independent.</p></section>
<section><h2>The governing exchange</h2><p class="quote">Here is what I think you have now seen.</p><p class="quote">Yes — but look again here.</p><p class="research-copy">That exchange is not preamble. It is part of the research apparatus.</p></section>
</BaseLayout>